{
  "abstract": "The existence of a directed strongly regular graph with parameters $(98,20,9,8,3)$ remains open. We study an entire class of eight equal rows of $B=A+I$. Its remaining thirteen closed out-neighbors carry a functional map, and we assume that exactly three of its basins have even cycles, counting all attached trees. A centered-signature rank identity and an exact finite classification show that every dependency of the outside boundary rows has coefficient sum zero. If that boundary has rank ten, all three even basins are balanced. If it has rank eleven, exactly two basins are balanced, with size pairs $(2,2)$, $(4,2)$, $(6,2)$, or $(4,4)$. In the latter case the unique function $\\alpha$ in the column space of $B$ that equals one on the eight-vertex class and zero on its other closed out-neighbors has total seven and is half-integral. The proof combines a column-space maximum principle with integral or half-integral preimages for unsigned functional incidence matrices. A rooted-tree construction gives a further restriction when $\\alpha$ is nonintegral and no odd basin occurs: only two balanced pairs and an unbalanced basin with sign populations $(5,4)$ remain, and the four balanced vertices send no arcs into the original class. A standalone standard-library supplement verifies the finite certificates and coverage, a complete 59-component inverse lemma, and exact column identities. The results include arbitrary attached trees and transpose to closed-column classes. They leave the rank-ten case, larger even-basin counts, smaller classes, and graph existence unresolved.",
